course: history
lesson: 30
title: The Industrial Revolution
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: From about 1760, first in Britain, people began making things with machines in big factories instead of by hand at home, and life changed more than at any time since the first farms.
explain: Steam engines, improved by James Watt, burned coal to boil water and the steam pushed pistons that drove spinning machines, trains and ships.
explain: Cloth became cheap, but mill and mine work was long and hard, and children as young as six worked in them, which is why reformers fought for laws that sent children to school instead.
explain: Think of how your gran's gran might have walked for two days to a city that you reach in two hours by train: railways, electric light, telephones, cars and planes all arrived within about 150 years.
explain: The factories also filled the air with smoke and dirtied rivers, the start of the pollution problems we still deal with today.
fact: The Industrial Revolution began in Britain around 1760, when people began making things with machines in factories instead of by hand at home.
fact: James Watt greatly improved the steam engine; he did not invent it, as earlier steam engines already pumped water out of mines.
fact: Steam engines burned coal to boil water, and the steam pushed pistons that powered factories, trains and ships.
fact: Spinning and weaving machines in big cotton mills made cloth much faster and cheaper, so ordinary people could afford more clothes.
fact: Children as young as six worked long hours in mills and mines, and in 1833 a British law banned children under nine from working in most textile mills.
fact: The first public steam railway opened in England in 1825, and in 1830 the Liverpool and Manchester Railway began running.
fact: People moved from villages to work in factory towns, and cities like Manchester grew fast, crowded, smoky and dirty, which later led to sewers, clean water and parks.
fact: Workers formed trade unions to ask together for fair pay, shorter hours and safety, and won things like the weekend and the eight-hour day.
fact: Michael Faraday learned how to make electricity with magnets in 1831, and Thomas Edison, Joseph Swan and others made electric light practical around 1880.
fact: Before telephones, messages went by letter, by messenger, or from the 1840s by telegraph, coded clicks sent along wires; Alexander Graham Bell made a working telephone in 1876, and Guglielmo Marconi sent radio signals across the Atlantic in 1901.
fact: Karl Benz built one of the first cars in 1885, and in 1903 the Wright brothers made the first powered aeroplane flight, which lasted 12 seconds.
fact: Smoke from factories and coal fires dirtied the air and rivers, and the problem of pollution, and later of climate change, began with the Industrial Revolution.
exercise: Where did the Industrial Revolution begin? | In Britain | In China, in the south | In Brazil | In Mali
exercise: About when did the Industrial Revolution begin? | Around 1760 | Around the year 1066 | Around the year 1960 | Around the year 500
exercise: What fuel did steam engines burn? | Coal | Petrol | Electricity | Sunlight
exercise: What did James Watt do? | He greatly improved the steam engine | He invented the very first steam engine from a kettle | He invented the car | He built the first plane
exercise: Why did clothes become cheaper? | Machines in mills made cloth faster | Cotton grew on every tree in Britain | Clothes were given out free by the king | People wore fewer clothes
exercise: How young were some children who worked in mills and mines? | Six years old | Eighteen years old | Only adults worked | Thirty years old
exercise: Why do children go to school instead of factories today? | Reformers won laws to limit child work and send children to school | Factories do not like children and never did | Children asked to stop | Machines are far too big and heavy for small children to work
exercise: When did the first public steam railway open in England? | 1825 | 1066 | 1950 | 1492
exercise: How did railways change travel? | Journeys of days took only hours | Journeys became much slower and more expensive than before | Only kings could travel | People stopped travelling
exercise: Why did cities grow so fast? | People moved there to work in factories | Villages were banned by law | The countryside was flooded by the rivers and the sea | Cities paid people to live there
exercise: How did workers win the weekend? | By joining together in trade unions | By asking the king very nicely, just once, in a letter | By working longer hours | By inventing the train
exercise: Who learned to make electricity with magnets in 1831? | Michael Faraday | Isaac Newton | James Cook | Karl Benz, the car maker
exercise: Who made a working telephone in 1876? | Alexander Graham Bell | Thomas Edison | Marie Curie | Charles Darwin, who sailed the world
exercise: Who sent radio messages across the Atlantic in 1901? | Guglielmo Marconi | Leonardo da Vinci | Galileo Galilei | James Watt
exercise: How long was the Wright brothers' first flight? | 12 seconds | 12 whole hours | 12 whole days | 12 long minutes
exercise: What problem did factories bring? | Smoke in the air and dirty rivers | Too much clean water in the rivers and lakes | Too many forests | Silence in the cities
test: everyone knows watt invented the steam engine watching a kettle boil. yeah? | No, he improved engines that already existed | Yes, he got the idea from his mum's kettle | Yes, in 1066 | No, Newton invented it
test: Edison invented the light bulb all by himself. Is that the full story? | No, many people, such as Joseph Swan, worked on it | Yes, entirely alone, in one night | No, the Romans already had glass light bulbs in their villas | Yes, in the Middle Ages
test: A family in 1700 makes cloth by hand at home. In 1830 the father works where? | In a big mill full of machines | In a castle | On a spaceship | Still only at home, by hand, near the fire
test: Why do you get Saturday and Sunday off school and many grown-ups get them off work? | Workers fought together for the weekend | It was always like that, since Roman times | A computer chose it | Railways needed two days to rest
test: What would happen to a factory town with no sewers or clean water? | People would get sick more often | Nothing, dirt is harmless | The factories would make more cloth | The town would move itself
test: Grandad says the first plane flew for an hour. What's right? | The Wright brothers' first flight lasted 12 seconds | It flew for a whole week | It flew round the world without landing even once to take on fuel | Planes came before trains
test: Why did coal matter so much to the Industrial Revolution? | It fuelled the steam engines | It was used to make cloth | It was eaten by workers | It replaced bread
test: mate wants to know how ppl sent messages before phones. | By letter, messenger or telegraph | By text message | By email | They couldn't send any
test: A kid of eight in 1830 in a mill and a kid of eight today. What is the biggest difference? | Today the child goes to school, not to work | Both work twelve hours a day | Today's child works in a mine | No difference at all
test: Why did the Industrial Revolution start the problem of climate change? | Burning coal began to add lots of smoke and gases to the air | It cooled the Sun | Trains melted the ice | It has nothing to do with it; the weather was just the same back then
exam: Identify the nation in which the Industrial Revolution commenced, circa seventeen hundred and sixty. | Great Britain | France | China | The United States
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of James Watt? | He substantially improved the existing steam engine | He invented the first steam engine upon observing a kettle | He constructed the first motor car | He discovered electricity
exam: Ascertain the duration of the first powered aeroplane flight undertaken by the Wright brothers in nineteen hundred and three. | Twelve seconds | Twelve whole minutes | Twelve long hours | Two entire days
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the attainment of the weekend by labourers. | It was secured through collective action by trade unions | It was conferred by the Church in the Middle Ages | It was a consequence of the invention of the telephone | It has always existed
